---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { lifeBits } from '../lib/life-bits';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Background & approach — Jirka Schlesinger"
  description="UX Lead at PwC — how I approach work, skills and focus areas, career background, and a little context outside work."
  path="/about/"
>
  <article class="prose about-page">
    <header class="about-page__hero">
      <h1 class="about-page__title">Background & approach</h1>
      <p class="role-detail">PwC · Prague</p>
    </header>

    <section class="about-page__section about-page__section--approach" aria-labelledby="approach-heading">
      <h2 id="approach-heading">How I approach UX work</h2>
      <div class="about-panel">
        <p>I see UX as helping people make better decisions when the problem is still taking shape, time is limited, and constraints are real.</p>
        <p>That usually means:</p>
        <ul class="about-approach-list">
          <li>slowing down early enough to frame the problem well</li>
          <li>asking direct questions while options are still open</li>
          <li>making assumptions and trade‑offs explicit</li>
          <li>keeping delivery realities in view as design moves forward</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          With AI‑enabled systems, that discipline matters even more. Usability, transparency, and trust need to be designed together from the start.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="skills-heading" class="skill-grid about-page__section about-page__section--skills">
      <div class="about-section-intro">
        <h2 id="skills-heading">Skills & focus areas</h2>
        <p class="muted small skills-intro">
          Grouped for scanning. Ask me how any of these areas showed up on a specific engagement.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="skills-matrix">
        <div class="skill-group">
          <h3>UX, product & ownership</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>UX leadership</li>
            <li>End‑to‑end UX ownership</li>
            <li>Product design</li>
            <li>Problem framing</li>
            <li>Interaction design</li>
            <li>Service design</li>
            <li>Design direction & review</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="skill-group">
          <h3>Discovery, research & decisions</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>User research (qualitative)</li>
            <li>Discovery in ambiguous problem spaces</li>
            <li>Insight synthesis toward decisions</li>
            <li>Assumption mapping & validation</li>
            <li>Workshop facilitation</li>
            <li>Decision framing</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="skill-group">
          <h3>AI & data‑heavy UX</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>AI UX design</li>
            <li>UX for AI‑enabled systems</li>
            <li>Responsible & trustworthy UX</li>
            <li>Explainability & transparency</li>
            <li>Data‑heavy & document‑intelligence experiences</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="skill-group">
          <h3>Enterprise, consulting & delivery</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Stakeholder management</li>
            <li>Enterprise & regulated environments</li>
            <li>Pragmatic UX strategy</li>
            <li>Delivery‑aware design</li>
            <li>Scope definition & shaping</li>
            <li>UX upselling & positioning</li>
            <li>Cross‑functional collaboration</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="about-page__section" aria-labelledby="background-heading">
      <div class="about-section-intro">
        <h2 id="background-heading">Current role & background</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="about-panels">
        <div class="about-panel">
          <h3>UX Lead — PwC</h3>
          <p class="muted small about-panel__meta">Prague · 10 years at PwC</p>
          <p>
            Ten years at PwC, from UX designer to UX Lead on complex digital and AI‑enabled products in a consulting environment — from discovery through delivery, with senior stakeholders,
            governance, and enterprise constraints in the room from the start. The work spans internal platforms and client-facing tools, with a focus on
            clear problem framing, practical design decisions, and solutions teams can build and maintain.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="about-panel">
          <h3>Education</h3>
          <p>
            Graphic School Prague — polygraphy track (2000–2004, graduated 2004). The curriculum centred on <strong>typography, layout, and visual hierarchy</strong>:
            building grids and spreads that hold up in print, treating type and imagery as a system, and preparing files so intent survives reproduction — colour,
            trapping, image handling, and finishing. That discipline translated early into <strong>information design</strong> and UI work: hierarchy isn’t decoration;
            it’s how people find their way. The same mindset carries into today’s handoffs — whether to a printer then or to engineering and a design system now.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="about-panel">
          <h3>Senior graphic designer — Dynamic Broadcast</h3>
          <p class="muted small about-panel__meta">Prague · May 2013 – December 2015</p>
          <p>
            I was the sole in-house designer in a small broadcast technology company. The job mixed client-facing work with our own product and marketing needs:
            branding and refreshes, web UI, and interfaces for video products used internationally — flows that had to work under real commercial pressure.
          </p>
          <p>
            Scope also covered internal and outbound communications: print (business cards through exhibition graphics), sales and technical PDFs, email templates,
            and day-to-day ownership of how we showed up visually on social channels so the message matched the product story.
          </p>
          <p>
            Representative output included the vuMedia / MediaLounge OTT front end, Visual Unity product and corporate touchpoints, Dynamic Broadcast’s own identity
            and web presence, and mobile and TV apps (Android TV, iOS, Android) — logos through to shipped UI. Details age quickly; ask if you want context on what
            survived and what I’d do differently today.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="about-panel">
          <h3>Volunteering — TEDxPrague</h3>
          <p class="muted small about-panel__meta">Prague · May 2015 · TEDxPragueWomen</p>
          <p>
            TEDxPrague invited me to help with graphics for TEDxPragueWomen: event programme, name badges, business cards, and presentation slides. Illustration by
            Tomski & Polanski — thanks to them and to the TEDxPrague team for the collaboration.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="about-page__section about-page__section--life" aria-labelledby="life-heading">
      <h2 id="life-heading">Outside work</h2>
      <ul class="life-bits life-bits--chips">
        {
          lifeBits.map((bit) => (
            <li>
              <span class="life-bits__icon" aria-hidden="true" set:html={bit.icon} />
              {bit.label}
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </section>

    <p class="about-page__footer-nav">
      <a href="/projects/">Projects</a>
      <span class="muted"> · </span>
      <a href="/">Home</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
